For finding the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, one efficient algorithm that can be used is the "Two-Pointer" approach. This approach involves using two pointers - one at the beginning of the array (let's call it `left`) and another at the end of the array (let's call it `right`). We then iterate through the array from both ends towards the center, comparing elements pointed to by the two pointers. If we find an element larger than the current largest element (`max`), we update `max`. If the current element is smaller than `max`, but larger than the current second-largest element (`second_max`), we swap these two values. Finally, once the loop completes, `second_max` will hold our answer.

Here's how this could look like in C++:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int getSecondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Initialize max and second-max with first two elements.
    int max = arr[0];
    int second_max = arr[1];

    // Traverse the array starting from index 2.
    for (int i = 2; i < n; i++) {
        if (arr[i] > max) {
            // Update max.
            second_max = max;
            max = arr[i];
        } else if (arr[i] > second_max && arr[i] != max) {
            // Update second_max.
            second_max = arr[i];
        }
    }

    return second_max;
}

int main()
{
    int arr[] = {5, 3, 8, 4, 9};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "The second largest number is: "
         << getSecondLargest(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

This implementation iterates over the entire array exactly twice, so its time complexity is O(n). It also handles edge cases well – if the array contains only one element, or no duplicates, it correctly returns either the maximum or the minimum value respectively.

To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array, we can use a sorting algorithm like quicksort or mergesort with a little modification. We will sort the array first, then pick out the second largest element.
Here's a sample implementation:
```
#include <algorithm>
using namespace std;
int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n)
{
    // Sort the array
    sort(arr, arr + n);

    // Return the second largest element
    return arr[n - 2];
}
```
This function takes an array `arr` of size `n` as input and returns the second largest element. It uses the standard library function `std::sort()` which sorts the elements in ascending order. Since the array is sorted in ascending order, the second largest element will be at index `n-2`.
The time complexity of this function is O(n log n), which is optimal since sorting requires a linearithmic number of operations (O(n log n)). However, if the array is already partially sorted or nearly sorted, a more efficient algorithm like bucket sort or counting sort could be used instead.

One algorithm that can be used to find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array is called "Selection Sort". This algorithm works by repeatedly finding the minimum (or maximum) element from the unsorted part of the array and placing it at the end of the sorted part of the array.
Here's an example implementation of this algorithm in C++:
```
