```
This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs, and returns the second largest element in the array. It uses two variables `max1` and `max2`, which initially store the smallest and second smallest elements respectively. Then, it iterates over each element in the array, updating these variables accordingly. Finally, it returns `max2`.
The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), since it makes one pass through the array and performs constant-time operations on each element.

To find the second largest integer element in an unsorted array in C++, we can use a modified version of the Quickselect algorithm. This algorithm is similar to the quicksort algorithm but instead of selecting a pivot to partition the array, it selects a pivot and then recursively applies the same operation on one half of the array until the desired number of elements have been found.

Here's a C++ implementation of this algorithm:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
using namespace std;

int findSecondLargest(int arr[], int n) {
    if (n == 0 || n == 1)
        return -1; // If there are no elements or only one element

    int left = 0;
    int right = n - 1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        // Check if the middle element is the first largest
        if (arr[mid] > arr[right]) {
            if ((mid != 0 && arr[mid - 1] >= arr[mid])
                || (mid == 0 && arr[0] >= arr[right])) {
                return arr[mid];
            }
            else {
                left = mid + 1;
            }
        }

        // Check if the last element is the first largest
        else if (arr[left] > arr[right]) {
            if ((left != 0 && arr[left - 1] >= arr[left])
                || (left == 0 && arr[0] >= arr[left])) {
                return arr[left];
            }
            else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }

        // Move towards the end of the array
        else {
            right--;
        }
    }

    return -1; // Return -1 if the array does not contain two distinct elements
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {45, 36, 87, 99, 78};
    int n = sizeof(arr)/sizeof(arr[0]);

    cout << "The second largest element is: "
         << findSecondLargest(arr, n);

    return 0;
}
```

This function takes an array `arr` and its size `n` as inputs and returns the second largest element in the array. It uses a recursive approach with the divide-and-conquer technique to efficiently find the target element. The time complexity of this algorithm is O(n), which makes it efficient enough for large arrays.
